                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "present_simple_continuous",
                    "title": "Present simple vs present continuous",
                    "explanation": "El present simple habla de hábitos, rutinas y cosas que siempre son verdad:\n- «You usually leave your keys on the table.»\n- «Priya works at the hospital.»\n\nEl present continuous habla de lo que pasa ahora mismo y de situaciones temporales:\n- «I'm making toast.»\n- «This week I'm starting at eight.»\n\nAlgunos verbos casi nunca van en continuous porque expresan estados, no acciones: «need», «want», «like», «love», «know», «understand». Se dice «I need a coffee», nunca «I'm needing a coffee».",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Present simple",
                        "Present continuous"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Uso",
                          "hábitos, rutinas, verdades",
                          "ahora, situaciones temporales"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Pistas",
                          "always, usually, every day, on Mondays",
                          "now, at the moment, today, this week"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Ejemplo",
                          "You usually leave your keys here.",
                          "I'm looking for my keys."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "You don't start until nine.",
                          "I'm not drinking it."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Sam usually takes Priya's milk.",
                      "Look! Sam's making toast.",
                      "This week we're working on a big project.",
                      "I need more sleep!"
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "phrasal_verbs",
                    "title": "Everyday phrasal verbs",
                    "explanation": "Un phrasal verb es un verbo + una partícula («up», «on», «off», «for», «out»…) que juntos tienen un significado propio: «look» (mirar) → «look for» (buscar).\n\nLos más usados en la rutina:\n- «wake up» (despertarse), «get up» (levantarse)\n- «put on» (ponerse ropa), «take off» (quitarse)\n- «turn on» / «turn off» (encender / apagar)\n- «look for» (buscar), «go out» (salir), «come back» (volver)\n\nSe conjugan como el verbo normal:\n- «I get up at seven.»\n- «She gets up at six.»\n- «I got up late yesterday.»\n\nCon «put on», «take off», «turn on» y «turn off» el objeto puede ir en medio. Si es un pronombre, va siempre en medio (nunca «put on it»):\n- «Put your coat on.» / «Put on your coat.»\n- «Put it on.»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Phrasal verb",
                        "Significado",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "wake up",
                          "despertarse",
                          "I wake up at half past six."
                        ],
                        [
                          "get up",
                          "levantarse",
                          "Sam never gets up before ten."
                        ],
                        [
                          "look for",
                          "buscar",
                          "I'm looking for my keys."
                        ],
                        [
                          "put on / take off",
                          "ponerse / quitarse",
                          "Take off my slippers, please!"
                        ],
                        [
                          "turn on / turn off",
                          "encender / apagar",
                          "Turn off the light, please."
                        ],
                        [
                          "go out / come back",
                          "salir / volver",
                          "We're going out. We'll come back at ten."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I'm looking for my keys. Have you seen them?",
                      "You're wearing my slippers! Take them off!",
                      "Sam turns on the radio when he makes breakfast.",
                      "This week I get up at seven because I start at eight."
                    ]
                  }
                },
